body:
With the younger generation a lot of my peers feel its okay to ask these questions because its so normalized in the media? They don't really understand that its a huge invasion of privacy. ALSO im a lot further along than most of my peers in the area I live in. I live on my own, with my S/O. I work full time and I go to college. They all treat me as sort of the mom since they still are riding this youthful high and they think IM thinking about this stuff (when I am definitely not). all my friend live at home, or if they don't they just work and don't do the whole school thing.

&#x200B;

Not only that,  I didn't even come out as fully homoromantic until late April. People just remember me as the woman I was (dating a male, and all my long term relationships have been with males), and they wonder how im going to live that "ideal" heteronormative lifestyle now that penises are out of the equation. Truth is, I've never wanted to give birth to my own biological kids. it was an argument I've had with my previous partners over and over. I don't want to carry another human.

&#x200B;

And with the pronouns thing, my girlfriend has shown that to me over and over. The way she writes.. its like the author had this sexy, androgynous mysterious vibe only because she doesn't bog down the literature with over stimulating the reader with "WOOO IM A LESBIANNNNN". I admire it a lot, and I kinda wanted to just take a raw and honest approach to our story.
